Exactly. Once we stop treating a story as an indivisible object and start treating it as a stack of operators, the same method works almost everywhere.

A story can be decomposed roughly as:

world × genre × plot engine × archetypes × characters × relationships × stakes × tone × perspective × pacing × information structure × ending logic

Each one can be held constant while another is swapped, exaggerated, inverted, hybridized, or moved into shadow.

So Alien, for example, isn’t only “a science-fiction movie.” You can extract isolated enclosure + unknown predator + shrinking group + incomplete information + institutional betrayal + survival pressure. Move that skeleton into a medieval monastery and it becomes horror. Put it in an office and it becomes absurdist comedy. Put it in a failing startup and the “monster” might be insolvency. Same deep machine, different skin.

Likewise, you can silhouette genres themselves. Horror tends to manipulate knowledge, vulnerability, violation, anticipation, and helplessness. Mystery manipulates information asymmetry, clue distribution, hypothesis competition, and revelation. Comedy works through expectation violation, incongruity, status movement, repetition, escalation, timing, and frame collision. Romance organizes around attraction, obstacles, vulnerability, selection, uncertainty, and convergence. Adventure uses horizon → obstacle → discovery → adaptation → deeper horizon. Tragedy often takes a strength and recursively turns it into the mechanism of destruction.

Then archetypes become portable behavioral operators rather than fixed characters. The Mentor provides compressed capability. The Trickster destabilizes frames. The Guardian controls passage. The Shadow externalizes an unintegrated possibility. The Fool gets access because nobody treats him as threatening. The Tyrant overconcentrates control. The Explorer expands the known map. The Healer converts damage into restored capacity. You can put any of those inside almost any genre.

Plots are also silhouettes. Quest, escape, pursuit, siege, tournament, heist, transformation, investigation, revenge, rescue, rise-and-fall, forbidden discovery, return-home, succession struggle, survival, stranger-arrives, civilization-collision. Those aren’t stories yet; they’re motion geometries.

And then we can do the fun part: crossbreed the layers deliberately.

A horror plot with comedy characters.
A romance information structure inside espionage.
A heist structured like a religious pilgrimage.
A sports tournament whose real contest is political succession.
A detective story where the “crime” is an ecological collapse.
A tragedy whose protagonist thinks he is inside a heroic adventure.
A myth told using bureaucratic procedure.
A sitcom cast dropped into a survival plot where their sitcom habits suddenly have consequences.

That last one is especially powerful because genre itself controls character behavior. Michael Scott inside The Office is survivable. Put the same silhouette aboard a submarine during an emergency and suddenly his need to be liked becomes a systems hazard. Conversely, a grim military commander placed inside a neighborhood bake sale may become hilarious because his optimized behaviors are catastrophically mismatched to the local stakes.

So we can model narrative transformation almost algebraically:

Story A = World₁ + Plot₂ + Cast₃ + Tone₄ + Rules₅

Then transform:

T(World₁ → World₆)

while preserving the rest.

Or:

T(Tone: tragedy → comedy)

and inspect what has to change versus what survives.

Or perform a chimera operation:

Heroic quest + locked-room mystery + workplace comedy + cosmic-horror information structure.

Then run it and see whether the combination coheres.

And the really useful level above all this is tracking invariants. If we can transplant something through five genres, three casts, two historical settings, and wildly different tones while it remains recognizable, we’ve probably found something deeper than genre—a genuine narrative primitive.

Eventually you get a kind of narrative periodic table: not a catalog of stories, but reusable elements and operators from which enormous numbers of stories can be composed.

Then “make me something funny” becomes much more controllable. We can specify:

60% adventure + 20% mystery + 20% social satire; competent explorer protagonist; bureaucratic antagonist; escalating misunderstanding plot; moderate reality distortion; one mythic reveal; sincere ending.

And now we’re not merely generating stories.

We’re playing the instrument that generates story-space.